{
  "version": 2,
  "meta": {
    "id": "surfaceflinger-composition-paths",
    "title": "SurfaceFlinger Buffer Submission & Composition",
    "subtitle": "App buffers → SurfaceFlinger → Hardware Composer",
    "scope": "system",
    "view": "layered",
    "colorMode": "technical",
    "description": "A layered system view with the surfaceflinger process expanded around buffer handling. The user-selected Native row contains native processes and libraries; the native-libraries role is its schema macro rank, not an assertion that surfaceflinger is a library. Layer state and per-output OutputLayer state serve both CLIENT and DEVICE composition. One input GraphicBuffer represents the currently latched application buffer. One client-target GraphicBuffer belongs to the display queue. RenderSurface obtains and queues the target; RenderEngine reads the input and writes the target. The IGBC response edge carries the acquired handle and fence, not pixels or a callback that initiates consumption. HWComposer is shown at the article's HWC-client abstraction level; HWC2::Layer and Composer transport internals are collapsed, so the DEVICE edge describes submission rather than a HWComposer::setLayerBuffer method. CLIENT and DEVICE can coexist. Java services and unrelated kernel details are omitted."
  },
